{{!--
  Eiserne Zeit — the generic card, front.

  Front and back are one construction: title head, body, card-kind foot.
  Every face of an overflowing card carries all three.

    ┌────────────────────────────────┐
    │ ███ black head band, title ████│  .ez-band      (10 % of the height — half on later faces)
    │▀▚▄▀▀▄▄▀  torn edge  ▄▀▀▚▄▀▀▚▄▀│  .ez-torn
    │  card text as markdown       ║ │  .ez-body
    │         ▓ steel logo ▓       ║ │  .ez-logo-band (on the LAST face only)
    │▄▀▀▚▄▀▀▄  torn edge  ▚▄▀▀▄▀▚▄▀▀│  .ez-torn-foot
    │ ███  GEAR                  ███ │  .ez-foot      (every face)
    └────────────────────────────────┘
       ║ = .ez-side-ref, along the right edge

  The card knows ONLY a title, a picture, a source and a card kind as values.
  Everything that is content — stats, subtitles, headings, tables — the author
  writes as markdown in the note, and the body is one place, `front-body`,
  filled by `content` in the block or, failing that, the note's own text
  before its first heading. Open a note, write the card.

  The author steers the pagination the same way: what must stay together goes
  between `%% keep-together %%` and `%% /keep-together %%`, a hard break is
  `%% card-break %%`. Both are Obsidian comments, invisible in the note.

  The logo is the LAST block of the body, not a band below it — that is the
  whole trick. The splitter moves content from the end forward, so what comes
  last can land on no face but the last one; earlier faces never carry it and
  lose no room to it. The obvious alternative — a band under the body, shown
  on the last face by CSS — does NOT work: `.cs-front-has-next` and the other
  position markers are stamped AFTER the measure-split-scale pass, and content
  hanging on them would add height to the last face after its body was
  measured, clipping the text above it. Reserving the band on every face and
  hiding it with `visibility` would be correct but costs 17 % of the card
  height on every face — measured, and enough to push a dense card's command
  block off the front. The band is shown by `.cs-overflow-active` instead,
  which the splitter stamps BEFORE it measures; on a card that fits, the
  designed back is the last face and carries the logo itself.

  The band centres and sizes the logo by flex alone: `flex: 1 1 0` takes all
  the space left over. The back (back.hbs) builds the same nesting so that ONE
  stylesheet rule serves both faces.

  The page counter sits INSIDE the title's `.text-scalable` span, not beside
  it. The span is an inline-block: a wrapped title makes it as wide as the
  whole box, and a counter next to it would find no room and drop onto a line
  of its own — a two-word name printed as a three-line head. Inside the span
  it runs in the same inline flow and settles behind the last word. The font
  scaler measures the span, so the counter is counted; a card that fits never
  shows it.
--}}
<div class="card-root card-front eiserne-zeit generic-card">

  <div class="ez-head">
    <div class="ez-band">
      <div class="card-title"><span class="text-scalable">{{slot "head-title" plain=true}}<span class="cs-overflow-counter"></span></span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ez-torn"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card-content-container">
    <div class="card-body-scalable">

    {{#if (slot "front-image")}}
    {{> card-image url=(slot "front-image" image=true) alt="" placement="front"}}
    {{/if}}

    {{#if (slot "front-body")}}
    <div class="ez-body">{{slot "front-body" markdown="block"}}</div>
    {{/if}}

    <div class="ez-logo-band">
      <img class="ez-logo" src="{{asset "assets/eiserne-zeit-logo.png"}}" alt="" />
    </div>

    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ez-foot">
    <span class="ez-cont-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <div class="ez-torn ez-torn-foot"></div>
    <div class="ez-foot-label"><span class="text-scalable">{{slot "foot-label" plain=true fallback=(t "back-label")}}</span></div>
  </div>

  {{#if (slot "side-reference")}}
  <div class="ez-side-ref">{{slot "side-reference" markdown=false}}</div>
  {{/if}}

</div>
